// An intermediate package. It declares no messages, pipes or services - it
// exists to hold rules about its descendants, which is what makes it the
// subsystem boundary.
//
// Declaring an intermediate package is optional. Undeclared, it simply carries
// no rules.

package acme.retail

// Dependency direction for the whole subsystem, stated once in the one place
// that can see both sides of every relationship.
//
// Rank is declaration order, lowest first. A package may depend within its own
// tier or on any lower tier, never upward.
//
// A package is a place in the name tree; a tier is a rank constraining which way
// dependencies may point between packages. Not a second hierarchy - a constraint
// over the one that exists.
//
//   acme.retail.sales     -> acme.retail.ticketing   ok   (channel -> domain, downward)
//   acme.retail.sales     -> acme.retail.common      ok   (channel -> platform, downward)
//   acme.retail.ticketing -> acme.retail.sales       ERROR tier-violation
//   acme.retail.common    -> acme.retail.ticketing   ERROR tier-violation

tier platform { acme.retail.common }
tier domain   { acme.retail.ticketing }
tier channel  { acme.retail.sales }

